 and death  so for our committee the first data  analytic metric that we wanted to look  at was who is our key stakeholder who  could we identify as our champion  partner based on the frequency of blood  cultures ordered and you can see here  very quickly on the graph the standout  order number one ordering department is  the emergency department the ed  represents 70 of the orders followed by  internal medicine at almost 18 percent  [Music]  based on the fact that the ed was our  number one blood culture user we wanted  to take a deeper analytical dive and  look at some data metrics regarding  positive blood cultures and what  pathogens were identified in a six-month  period and that period of time was from  january 2021 to  june 2021  the results were that  there was a total  one  of  and three blood cultures ordered by the  ed  and of those four thousand one hundred  and three blood cultures  248 blood cultures were positive for a  pathogen  during the this six-month time period  the blood culture contamination rate was  1.75  or represented four blood cultures  leaving us with 244  positive blood cultures  of these 244 positive blood cultures we  wanted to see how many of these positive  blood cultures could have detected  a bacterial organism using the t2  bacterial panel  a review of the data reviewed that 142  blood cultures could have been  identified as having a  pathogen on the t2 panel  so our analysis further revealed that 58  of the total positive blood cultures  could have been rapidly identified  with one of the five sepsis causing  pathogens using the t2  bacterial panel with a single blood  tube collection  also the t2 results would have been  available to college clinician at least  24 hours earlier  than the traditional blood culture  results  and there would not be any interference  from antibiotics on board the patient at  the time of drawer  and third we could initiate targeted  antibiotic treatment at least 24 hours  sooner which can lead to high value  patient outcomes  i know this was one of the poll  questions in the beginning  asking you if you know what is the most  common pathogen in your organization and  i have to tell you that before this  retrospective review  it was not really clear as to which  pathogens we were actually seeing and  you can see here that um the breakdown  of those 142 ed positive blood cultures  it actually represented 88 unique  patients and our number one pathogen was  e coli followed by staph aureus in mrsa  followed by  klebsiella pneumoniae pseudomonas  originosa  and no one really thought that we saw  much enterococcus fascium but we  actually had two patients so it really  brought to the surface what we were  seeing in our specific patient  population  so my question to you is  who wouldn't want to know if a patient  has an escape pathogen in three to five  hours with a single blood drawer  if we look at performance  characteristics for the gold standard  which has traditionally been the blood  culture and compared to magnetic  resonance technology you can see that  the magnetic resonance technology with a  sensitivity of 90 percent in specificity  of 98  outperforms the traditional blood  culture  the sensitivity of blood culture is  overall low at 50 percent and that's  partly because blood culture depends on  the blood volume drawn  the timing of the blood drawer whether  the patient has had any prior treatment  with antibiotics as well as the presence  of a viable organism  additionally contamination results  are responsible for more than 1.4  million patients per year having full  positive blood culture results and this  leads to misdiagnosis which then can  lead to unnecessary antibiotic treatment  extended hospital stays and increased  mortality and cost  the value of the t2  i'm sorry  shown here is the recommendations from  our choosing wisely committee  and we chose to target high-risk  patients who are presenting to the  emergency room so to start off our  journey at our institution ed clinicians  may order a t2 bacterial panel for  patients who are presenting with  clinical signs and symptoms of blood  stream infection and or sepsis or at  risk of progression to sepsis  and starting empiric therapy  that also must be followed up by  infectious disease consultation  so we believe that the value of the t2  bacterial panel is unquestionable and  that it saves lives and dollars  if we look at the cost of 140  to the laboratory the value that we  receive back is that we have rapid  direct genus and species identification  with no antibiotic interference in less  than three to five hours with a single  blood drawer  as you heard from dr  schneider this also promotes antibiotic  stewardship  it then allows for de-escalation of  antibiotics by the pharmd in  consultation with clinicians  and it enhances patient care management  by changing broad spectrum antibiotics  to targeted antibiotic therapy thereby  improving patient outcomes and providing  high value outcomes for our patients  well what lessons have we learned well  there are many and there's even been  some bumps along the way but first it's  paramount to continue to have assessment  of all of our initiatives otherwise  progress and performance can drop off  significantly it's also important to  keep lines of communication open which  is critical between all stakeholders  we also have found that data analytics  play an essential role in everything we  do  and additionally that information  technology can assist with hard wiring  of changes for sustained use and  successful metrics  we also realize that change is slower  than expected especially in a covered  world today  and it's important to have patience and  recognize and celebrate the winds  as i said collaboration among all  stakeholders of paramount for success  and understanding everyone's strengths  and weaknesses is also important  who better knows the strengths and  weaknesses of our tests than you the  lab